(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-asthma-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Asthma Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which features define intermittent asthma?","choices":["Symptoms over 2 days a week, night waking 3 to 4 times a month","Daily symptoms, night waking more than once a week","Symptoms throughout the day, night waking often 7 times a week","Symptoms 2 or fewer days a week, night waking 2 or fewer times a month"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Intermittent asthma has symptoms and rescue use on 2 or fewer days a week, night awakenings 2 or fewer times a month, no interference with normal activity, and normal lung function between exacerbations. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"When features of a patient's asthma fall into different severity categories, which category is assigned?","choices":["The least severe category with any feature","The category set by lung function alone","The most severe category with any feature","The category that holds most of the features"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severity is assigned to the most severe category in which any feature occurs, after assessing impairment over the previous 2 to 4 weeks with spirometry. One severe feature is enough to move the patient up. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Below what forced expiratory volume in 1 second is bronchial provocation testing not recommended?","choices":["40% predicted","65% predicted","80% predicted","90% predicted"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bronchial provocation deliberately triggers bronchospasm with methacholine or histamine, so it is not recommended if the forced expiratory volume in 1 second is below 65% predicted. Below that, the airway has too little reserve. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is cardiac asthma?","choices":["Asthma triggered by a beta blocker","Palpitations from short-acting bronchodilators","Asthma that causes right heart strain","Wheezing caused by decompensated heart failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac asthma is wheezing caused by decompensated heart failure rather than by asthma itself. A new wheeze in a patient with heart disease should prompt evaluation of the heart, not just bronchodilators. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which early-life infection may be associated with later development of asthma?","choices":["Streptococcal pharyngitis in childhood","Childhood exposure to farm germs","Respiratory syncytial virus in infancy","Aspergillus fumigatus in infancy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Early viral infection, particularly respiratory syncytial virus in infancy, may have an association with later asthma development. In someone who already has asthma, the same virus is also a trigger of exacerbations. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which finding is listed as a toxic effect of theophylline?","choices":["Hyperkalemia","Hypoglycemia","Seizure","Oral candidiasis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Theophylline toxic effects include nausea, vomiting, tachyarrhythmias, headache, seizure, hyperglycemia and hypokalemia; insomnia, dyspepsia and reflux are its ordinary side effects. Its narrow toxic-therapeutic range makes it rarely used. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which description defines asthma?","choices":["Restrictive disorder that limits filling of the lungs","Chronic inflammatory airway disorder with reversible obstruction","Chronic infection of the airways with fixed obstruction","Destruction of alveolar walls with permanent air trapping"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asthma is a chronic inflammatory airway disorder whose airflow obstruction is reversible, producing recurrent breathlessness and wheezing and difficulty getting air out of the lungs. Some long-standing cases develop irreversible obstruction, but reversibility is the defining feature. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Why should a long-acting beta-2 agonist not be used as monotherapy in asthma?","choices":["It causes oral candidiasis","It cannot be given by inhaler","It has no anti-inflammatory effect","It lasts too short a time to help"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A long-acting beta-2 agonist such as salmeterol or olodaterol bronchodilates for up to 12 hours but has no anti-inflammatory effect, so it is combined with an inhaled corticosteroid, often allowing control at a lower steroid dose. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which group of processes limits air movement in asthma?","choices":["Bronchoconstriction, airway edema, and vascular congestion","Alveolar destruction, bullae, and loss of recoil","Pleural fibrosis, chest wall stiffness, and weakness","Pulmonary edema, alveolar fluid, and cardiomegaly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asthma limits air movement through bronchoconstriction, airway edema and vascular congestion, with occlusion of the airway lumen by secretions in some cases. All of these narrow the airway itself, which is why the obstruction can reverse. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Between exacerbations, the physical examination of a patient with asthma is often what?","choices":["Marked by digital clubbing","Marked by a barrel chest","Normal","Marked by fixed wheezing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The examination may be normal between exacerbations, which is why an objective lung function measurement is needed for the diagnosis. Extrinsic asthma may still show nasal mucosal swelling, increased secretions, eczema or nasal polyps. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which signs show increased work of breathing during an asthma exacerbation?","choices":["Facial flushing, rhinorrhea, and conjunctival irritation","Clubbing, cyanosis, and edema of the ankles","Accessory muscle use, leaning forward, hunched shoulders","Slow deep breaths with a relaxed posture"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Increased work of breathing during an exacerbation shows as accessory muscle use, leaning forward and hunched shoulders. These signs mean the patient is struggling to move air through narrowed airways. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How is exercise-induced bronchospasm best prevented?","choices":["Regular inhaled corticosteroid use","Avoiding exercise entirely","Oral theophylline before games","A long-acting beta-2 agonist alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Exercise-induced bronchospasm is treated with an inhaled short-acting beta-2 agonist before exercise and is best prevented with regular inhaled corticosteroid use. It is common in children and adolescents, so this keeps teenagers in sport. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Airflow reduction in asthma lowers which set of measurements?","choices":["Total lung capacity, residual volume, and diffusion","Arterial oxygen, carbon dioxide, and bicarbonate","Inspiratory flow, tidal volume, and breathing rate","Expiratory volume in 1 second, its ratio, and peak flow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Reduced airflow lowers the forced expiratory volume in 1 second, the ratio of that volume to forced vital capacity, and the peak expiratory flow. These are all expiratory measures, matching asthma's difficulty getting air out. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which feature raises the risk of death from asthma?","choices":["Onset of symptoms before age 3","Regular use of an inhaled corticosteroid","Symptoms that resolve during adolescence","Previous admission for a near-fatal attack"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Risk of death rises with poorly controlled asthma (frequent bronchodilator use), no inhaled corticosteroid or poor compliance with it, and previous admissions for near-fatal attacks. A past near-fatal attack is one of the listed markers. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which spirometry pattern before a bronchodilator signals airflow limitation in asthma?","choices":["Reduced 1-second volume and reduced ratio","Normal ratio, reduced vital capacity","Increased 1-second volume","Normal values, lower after dilator"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Before a bronchodilator, airflow limitation shows as a reduced forced expiratory volume in 1 second and a reduced ratio of that volume to forced vital capacity. Improvement after the bronchodilator then supports asthma. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Asthma deaths are declining in affluent countries because of the widespread use of which therapy?","choices":["Allergen immunotherapy","Inhaled corticosteroids","Short-acting bronchodilators","Oral theophylline"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Death from asthma is uncommon and declining in affluent countries, credited to widespread use of inhaled corticosteroids, the anti-inflammatory controller. The flip side is that having no inhaled corticosteroid, or poor compliance with it, raises the risk of death. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which statement about mast cell stabilizers such as cromolyn is accurate?","choices":["They are the preferred rescue medication","They are reserved for severe persistent asthma","They have a narrow toxic-therapeutic range","They do not relieve symptoms once present"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cromolyn and nedocromil inhibit mast cell activation and eosinophil recruitment; they prevent symptoms and improve function in mild persistent or exercise-induced asthma but do not relieve symptoms once they are present. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which task belongs to the initial asthma visit rather than a follow-up visit?","choices":["Assess and monitor asthma control","Adjust medications as needed","Review the action plan and update it","Assess asthma severity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The initial visit diagnoses asthma, assesses severity, starts medication and demonstrates its use, develops a written action plan and schedules follow-up. Follow-up visits then assess and monitor control, review compliance and adjust medications. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is the most common reason for poor symptom control in refractory asthma?","choices":["Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Exercise-induced bronchospasm","Non-compliance","Steroid-resistant asthma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Refractory asthma takes two forms, persistent symptoms with poor lung function or normal function with severe exacerbations; non-compliance is the most common reason for poor symptom control, and steroid-resistant asthma is very uncommon. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"During an asthma attack, a patient is using accessory muscles and leaning forward, yet no wheezing is heard. What does the missing wheeze most likely indicate?","choices":["Bronchodilator toxicity","A severe exacerbation","An attack that has ended","A cardiac cause of the attack"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Accessory muscle use and leaning forward show increased work of breathing, and wheezing may not be present in a severe exacerbation; the patient may have only decreased breath sounds with a prolonged expiratory phase. A quiet chest in a struggling patient signals severity, not improvement. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 36, 50, 51, 71"},{"q":"How is spirometry best performed when asthma is suspected?","choices":["Before and after a bronchodilator","After a week of oral corticosteroids","During sleep at 3 to 4 in the morning","After exercise to provoke symptoms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Spirometry measures the volume of air exhaled over time; the best results come from measuring before and after a short-acting bronchodilator, so reversibility can be shown. It is repeated annually to monitor progression. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which psychiatric conditions appear in the differential diagnosis of asthma?","choices":["Generalized anxiety and panic disorder","Bipolar disorder and schizophrenia","Depression and insomnia","Conversion and emotional laryngeal wheeze"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Psychiatric causes in the differential are conversion disorders, emotional laryngeal wheezing and, rarely, malingering or factitious disorder. They can mimic asthma, which is one reason an objective lung function test is part of the diagnosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"On an asthma action plan, which finding is a danger sign that calls for the hospital or an ambulance now?","choices":["Blue lips or fingernails","Peak flow at 85% of best","Cough that comes with exercise","Mild morning chest tightness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The danger signs on the action plan are trouble walking and talking because of shortness of breath, and blue lips or fingernails; the patient takes quick-relief medicine and goes to the hospital or calls an ambulance now. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which viruses are listed as triggers of asthma exacerbations?","choices":["Hepatitis B, hepatitis C, and immunodeficiency virus","Herpes simplex, varicella, and Epstein-Barr virus","Measles, mumps, and rubella","Influenza, rhinovirus, and other respiratory viruses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Viral infections trigger exacerbations by a poorly understood mechanism; the list is influenza, rhinovirus, respiratory syncytial virus, coronavirus and other upper respiratory viruses. This is why influenza vaccination is advised in asthma. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which statement about the course of asthma severity is accurate?","choices":["Severe asthma is usually severe from onset","Mild asthma usually becomes severe over a decade","Severity changes with each season for most patients","Severity is set by the first viral infection in adulthood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Severity rarely varies for a given patient: mild asthma rarely becomes severe, and severe asthma is usually severe at onset. Severity is thought to be largely genetic, which fits a stable course. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Asthma symptoms that occur at predictable times in the menstrual cycle are called which type?","choices":["Cardiac asthma","Catamenial asthma","Intrinsic asthma","Occupational asthma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Catamenial asthma is asthma whose symptoms occur at predictable times during the menstrual cycle. It is listed among the other types of asthma with occupational, cardiac, exercise-induced and aspirin-sensitive patterns. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which three conditions form the atopic triad?","choices":["Allergic rhinitis, asthma, and atopic dermatitis","Asthma, nasal polyps, and aspirin sensitivity","Asthma, eczema, and food poisoning","Allergic rhinitis, sinusitis, and conjunctivitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The atopic triad is allergic rhinitis, asthma and atopic dermatitis (eczema). Finding one prompts questions about the others, and in extrinsic asthma the exam may show swollen nasal mucosa, increased secretions or eczema even when the chest is clear. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which is a side effect of systemic corticosteroids?","choices":["Gastric ulceration","Oropharyngeal candidiasis","Hypokalemia with seizures","Dry mouth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Systemic corticosteroid side effects include truncal obesity, osteoporosis, bruising, gastric ulcerations, depression and hypertension, which is why long-term use is kept to the minimum dose for refractory disease. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What does fractional exhaled nitric oxide testing identify in asthma?","choices":["Hyperinflation","Airway inflammation","Allergen triggers","Bronchodilator reversibility"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fractional exhaled nitric oxide testing identifies airway inflammation. It sits with the chest radiograph and allergy testing among the other tests, while allergy skin or blood testing identifies the allergen triggers. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which antibody response drives the asthma symptoms of allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis?","choices":["An immunoglobulin M response","A vigorous immunoglobulin E and G response","An immunoglobulin A response at the mucosa","Absent antibodies with normal immunity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Exposure to Aspergillus causes a vigorous immunoglobulin E and immunoglobulin G antibody response, leading to acute asthma symptoms or an exacerbation in a patient with asthma or cystic fibrosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 65"}]};
